dback.md\n-rw-------  1 agent agent   17862 Nov 11 14:55 validation_report.json\n# Paper validation feedback\n\n- generated_at: 2026-11-11T14:55:46.510940+00:00\n- paper_dir: `work/results/t02/claude/20261111_085902/paper`\n- ok: `False`\n\n## Hard errors\n- agentic evidence audit failed: see work/results/t02/claude/20261111_085902/paper/paper_evidence_audit_feedback.json and work/results/t02/claude/20261111_085902/paper/paper_evidence_audit.md\n- claims_grounding.json validation failed: claims[10].source_ids must be non-empty list; claims[13].source_ids must be non-empty list; claims[14].source_ids must be non-empty list; claims[15].source_ids must be non-empty list; claims[16].source_ids must be non-empty list\n\n## Supplementary Information validation\n- appendix_present: `True`\n- heading_present: `True`\n- placed_after_references: `True`\n- pre_bibliography_clearpage_present: `True`\n- clearpage_present: `True`\n- visible_character_count: `6075`\n- substantive_element_count: `10`\n- rendered_checked: `False`\n- rendered_heading_present: `False`\n- rendered_visible_character_count: `0`\n\n## Agentic evidence audit\n- checked: `True`\n- verdict: `fail`\n- finding_count: `3`\n- summary: Numbers, tables, figures and qualification facts match claims_grounding.json, paper_data.json and the evaluator sources; there are three narrow wording and sign inconsistencies, one of which makes a claim about when the decision rule was fixed that contradicts the Method section.\n\n### Audit findings to fix\n- `rule-declared-in-advance-1` (Introduction (paragraph 2) vs Method (Test statistic and decision rule)): The Introduction says the protocol fixed the three-way rule in advance. The Method says the rule is the authors' own operationalization, that the evaluator emits only the interval and metrics, and that no claim is made about when the rule was fixed. The artifacts do not show the rule was declared before the evaluation.\n  - claim: The protocol declares in advance that the prediction is refuted if the interval lies at or above zero and inconclusive if it straddles zero.\n  - evidence: paper.tex Introduction vs Method 'the rule is our own operationalization ... we make no claim about when the rule was fixed'; claims_grounding.json claim[3] says the evaluator 'retains no verdict logic'; source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py computes only the slope and its interval and has no verdict logic; derived/resolution.json#verdict_from_interval_rule is produced by scripts/derive_resolution.py.\n  - requested_fix: Rewrite the Introduction sentence to say the authors apply a three-way rule to the evaluator's reported interval, and remove 'declares in advance' and any implication that the rule was pre-specified.\n- `loglinear-sign-1` (Results, Resolution of the design (Bounded dynamic range) and Table 'resolution'): The slope is defined as the slope of ln(delay) on log2(width), so a falling delay gives a negative slope of about -0.922. The text and table report the unsigned magnitude as a slope, which contradicts the paper's own sign convention.\n  - claim: a delay falling log-linearly from the cap at d=16 to the floor at d=256 would give 0.922; table row 'Slope of a log-linear cap-to-floor profile = 0.922'\n  - evidence: derived/resolution.json#loglinear_full_range_slope = 0.9222 (magnitude of ln(40)/4); scripts/derive_resolution.py line 36 comment 'slope of a profile falling log-linearly from the cap at 16 to the floor at 256'; paper.tex defines the verdict via the slope of ln(delay) vs log2(width) with negative meaning delay shrinks.\n  - requested_fix: Write the value as -0.922, or say 'magnitude 0.922' in both the text and the table row.\n- `constants-no-tuning-1` (Supplementary Information, Evaluated model: constants and parameter counts): The source comments say offline selection or execution was not possible only for the output-scaling, unembedding-init and hidden-exponent defaults. The comments for the initial-weight scale (0.5) and the reference width (128) say nothing about tuning. The paper attributes the 'without offline tuning' statement to the comments for all of these constants.\n  - claim: The initial-weight scale (0.5), the unembedding scale (0.5), the reference width (128), and both exponents (0.5) are constants in the source; the code comments state they were chosen without offline tuning.\n  - evidence: source_evidence/frozen_model/program.py: the comment on _INIT_SCALE says only 'Omnigrok-style smaller initial weight norm'; the comment on REF_WIDTH, OUT_EXP and UNEMBED_INIT says 'offline selection was not possible in this stage'; the comment on HIDDEN_EXP says 'a mild choice made without offline execution'. The development-plane variants V1-V3 were scored before V3 was selected as the evaluated model.\n  - requested_fix: Limit the 'without offline tuning' statement to the output-scaling, unembedding-init and hidden-exponent defaults as the comments state. For the initial-weight scale and reference width, say only that they are source constants, and qualify 'not tuned on it' in the Method accordingly.\n# Draft reference gap memo\n\n**Status:** ready. A targeted post-draft retrieval pass is justified for three paragraphs. The rest of the draft is either well cited or grounded in `paper_data.json`.\n\n## What is already adequate\n- The grokking explanations in the introduction and the first Related Work paragraph (Power, Nanda, Liu, Varma, Kumar, Huang, Davies) are cited directly.\n- All quantitative results (slope, interval, censored fraction, resolution limits) are grounded in the evaluator artifacts and need no citations.\n\n## Targets\n\n### 1. Related work, \"Parametrization and optimizer scale\" (high, background)\nThe evaluated model's key design element is a width-dependent multiplier meant to equalize the effective step size under Adam. The Discussion argues this could mask or create a width slope. The paragraph cites only an RL normalization paper and a dynamical-richness measurement paper. The standard width-aware parametrization literature is missing.\n- Search topics: muP / Tensor Programs; hyperparameter transfer across width; Adam parametrization and effective step size.\n\n### 2. Related work and introduction, prior work on width dependence (high, positioning)\nThe paper asks how grokking delay depends on model width. No cited work is positioned as having examined delay against width, size, weight decay, or initialization scale. The \"Relation to this work\" paragraph and the capacity-based reading both need this positioning. The memorization and double-descent citations are only indirect support.\n- Search topics: grokking on modular arithmetic versus width and weight decay; grokking time versus model or data size; initialization scale and effective learning rate in grokking.\n\n### 3. Method, test statistic (medium, background)\nThe statistic is a seed-cluster percentile bootstrap on delays right-censored at the cap, entered at the cap value. The Discussion recommends censored-data analysis. No methodological reference supports either choice or the remedy.\n- Search topics: cluster bootstrap with few clusters; Tobit / accelerated-failure-time regression; bias from imputing censored values.\n\n## Not requested\nStronger citations for memorization, double descent, or the individual grokking explanations. These are adequately covered and would only enlarge the bibliography.\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f75a47ee-c82c-488f-8a2d-2df129d03c85","uuid":"2cd8f5a6-2ed7-47ec-a5c9-b421bf6cb3f4","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:55:52.295Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"total 12724\ndrwx------ 10 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 14:55 .\ndrwx------  9 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 14:45 ..\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     751 Nov 11 14:08 .agentic_paper_workspace.json\ndrwx------  3 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 14:08 .claude\n-rw-------  1 agent agent       0 Nov 11 14:54 .concept_figure_complete\nprw-------  1 agent agent       0 Nov 11 14:55 .paper_repair_round_4_stream_20261111_140816.fifo\n-rw-------  1 agent agent       0 Nov 11 14:08 .paper_two_stage_enabled\n-rw-------  1 agent agent       0 Nov 11 14:32 .postdraft_reference_enrichment_complete\n-rw-------  1 agent agent    3685 Nov 11 14:55 VENUE.md\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent   12013 Nov 11 14:53 claims_grounding.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent    6079 Nov 11 14:45 concept_figure_run.log\ndrwxr-xr-x  2 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 14:25 derived\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent    3721 Nov 11 14:26 draft_reference_gaps.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent    2303 Nov 11 14:26 draft_reference_gaps.md\n-rw-------  1 agent agent  132466 Nov 11 14:26 draft_reference_gaps_agent_claude_20261111_140816.lo