{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgwLN3QTB4ffZ5xstLmhj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0159Q6VMrWvHwGoTPqULodVv","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat paper_evidence_audit_feedback.md | tail -30; python3 - <<'E'\nimport json\nc=json.load(open('claims_grounding.json'))['claims']\nfor i,x in enumerate(c):\n    if i in (10,13,14,15,16) or not x.get('source_ids'): print(i,json.dumps(x)[:400])\nprint(len(c))\nE\ngrep -n \"in advance\\|declare\\|pre-declared\\|predeclared\\|pre-specified\\|prespecified\\|0\\.922\\|without offline\\|not tuned\\|tuned\" paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10548,"cache_read_input_tokens":36923,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10548},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f75a47ee-c82c-488f-8a2d-2df129d03c85","uuid":"607088b7-7172-4d5e-8d50-3b3e43cc0e14","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:55:55.315Z","request_id":"req_011CgwLN2pz7Lq1Bp3EFgAQs"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0159Q6VMrWvHwGoTPqULodVv","type":"tool_result","content":"# Agentic evidence audit feedback\n\n- generated_at: `2026-11-11T14:55:46.414137+00:00`\n- ok: `False`\n- verdict: `fail`\n- finding_count: `3`\n- audit_json_path: `work/results/t02/claude/20261111_085902/paper/paper_evidence_audit.json`\n- audit_markdown_path: `work/results/t02/claude/20261111_085902/paper/paper_evidence_audit.md`\n- raw_output_path: `work/results/t02/claude/20261111_085902/paper/paper_evidence_audit_raw.txt`\n\n## Summary\nNumbers, tables, figures and qualification facts match claims_grounding.json, paper_data.json and the evaluator sources; there are three narrow wording and sign inconsistencies, one of which makes a claim about when the decision rule was fixed that contradicts the Method section.\n\n## Findings\n- `rule-declared-in-advance-1` (Introduction (paragraph 2) vs Method (Test statistic and decision rule)): The Introduction says the protocol fixed the three-way rule in advance. The Method says the rule is the authors' own operationalization, that the evaluator emits only the interval and metrics, and that no claim is made about when the rule was fixed. The artifacts do not show the rule was declared before the evaluation.\n  - claim: The protocol declares in advance that the prediction is refuted if the interval lies at or above zero and inconclusive if it straddles zero.\n  - evidence: paper.tex Introduction vs Method 'the rule is our own operationalization ... we make no claim about when the rule was fixed'; claims_grounding.json claim[3] says the evaluator 'retains no verdict logic'; source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py computes only the slope and its interval and has no verdict logic; derived/resolution.json#verdict_from_interval_rule is produced by scripts/derive_resolution.py.\n  - requested_fix: Rewrite the Introduction sentence to say the authors apply a three-way rule to the evaluator's reported interval, and remove 'declares in advance' and any implication that the rule was pre-specified.\n- `loglinear-sign-1` (Results, Resolution of the design (Bounded dynamic range) and Table 'resolution'): The slope is defined as the slope of ln(delay) on log2(width), so a falling delay gives a negative slope of about -0.922. The text and table report the unsigned magnitude as a slope, which contradicts the paper's own sign convention.\n  - claim: a delay falling log-linearly from the cap at d=16 to the floor at d=256 would give 0.922; table row 'Slope of a log-linear cap-to-floor profile = 0.922'\n  - evidence: derived/resolution.json#loglinear_full_range_slope = 0.9222 (magnitude of ln(40)/4); scripts/derive_resolution.py line 36 comment 'slope of a profile falling log-linearly from the cap at 16 to the floor at 256'; paper.tex defines the verdict via the slope of ln(delay) vs log2(width) with negative meaning delay shrinks.\n  - requested_fix: Write the value as -0.922, or say 'magnitude 0.922' in both the text and the table row.\n- `constants-no-tuning-1` (Supplementary Information, Evaluated model: constants and parameter counts): The source comments say offline selection or execution was not possible only for the output-scaling, unembedding-init and hidden-exponent defaults. The comments for the initial-weight scale (0.5) and the reference width (128) say nothing about tuning. The paper attributes the 'without offline tuning' statement to the comments for all of these constants.\n  - claim: The initial-weight scale (0.5), the unembedding scale (0.5), the reference width (128), and both exponents (0.5) are constants in the source; the code comments state they were chosen without offline tuning.\n  - evidence: source_evidence/frozen_model/program.py: the comment on _INIT_SCALE says only 'Omnigrok-style smaller initial weight norm'; the comment on REF_WIDTH, OUT_EXP and UNEMBED_INIT says 'offline selection was not possible in this stage'; the comment on HIDDEN_EXP says 'a mild choice made without offline execution'. The development-plane variants V1-V3 were scored before V3 was selected as the evaluated model.\n  - requested_fix: Limit the 'without offline tuning' statement to the output-scaling, unembedding-init and hidden-exponent defaults as the comments state. For the initial-weight scale and reference width, say only that they are source constants, and qualify 'not tuned on it' in the Method accordingly.\n10 {\"claim\": \"Derived arithmetic from protocol constants (not from an evaluator metric): with delay floored at 250 and capped at 10,000 steps (ratio 40), OLS weights (-0.2,-0.1,0,0.1,0.2) over widths 16..256 give a maximum attainable |slope| of 0.3*ln(40)=1.107; a log-linear cap-to-floor profile gives 0.922.\", \"source_ids\": [], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_files\": [\"derived/resolution.json#max_abs_\n13 {\"claim\": \"Derived combinatorics from the four-seed design (not an evaluator metric): C(7,4)=35 distinct seed multisets; 4/4^4=1/64 of draws repeat one seed four times.\", \"source_ids\": [], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_files\": [\"derived/resolution.json#distinct_bootstrap_seed_multisets\", \"derived/resolution.json#bootstrap_draws_with_all_seeds_identical_prob\", \"source_evidence/workload/final_manif\n14 {\"claim\": \"Workload: modular addition modulo 97, 3,763 training and 5,646 held-out pairs, widths 16-256, 10,000 steps probed every 250, AdamW lr 1e-3, betas (0.9,0.98), weight decay 1.0, seeds 201-204; 2,000 bootstrap resamples and fit/generalization threshold 0.99 are read from the evaluator source.\", \"source_ids\": [], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_files\": [\"source_evidence/workload/final_manife\n15 {\"claim\": \"Parameter count 12d^2+(2p+9)d: 6,320 at d=16 to 838,400 at d=256 (derived arithmetic from the model source); multipliers (128/d)^0.5 = 2.83, 2.0, 1.41, 1.0, 0.71 (from source_evidence/frozen_model/program.py).\", \"source_ids\": [], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_files\": [\"source_evidence/frozen_model/program.py\", \"derived/resolution.json#params_by_width\", \"derived/resolution.json#hidden_a\n16 {\"claim\": \"Compute: 1,804 RPC calls (limit 2,200) over 22 episodes and 11,764 s elapsed in the final evaluation (source_evidence/final/final_manifest.json).\", \"source_ids\": [], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_files\": [\"source_evidence/final/final_manifest.json#compute_usage\", \"derived/resolution.json#compute\"]}\n33\n47:This paper reports a single, carefully scoped measurement of that kind. The setting is a one-layer transformer trained on modular addition modulo $p=97$ with a fixed training fraction, optimizer, and step limit, and the width $d$ is varied over $\\{16,32,64,128,256\\}$. The prediction under test is that the delay decreases with width, operationalized as the slope of $\\ln(\\text{delay})$ against $\\log_2 d$ being negative with a 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval entirely below zero. The protocol declares in advance that the prediction is \\emph{refuted} if the interval lies at or above zero and \\emph{inconclusive} if it straddles zero (Figure~\\ref{fig:overview}).\n103:The multiplier is $2.83, 2.0, 1.41, 1.0, 0.71$ at $d=16,\\dots,256$. The exponents ($0.5$) and the reference width ($128$) are fixed constants chosen before the final evaluation, not tuned on it. The training workload is dictated by the evaluator: AdamW with learning rate $10^{-3}$, $\\beta=(0.9,0.98)$, decoupled weight decay $1.0$, full-batch updates on all $3{,}763$ training pairs, and $10{,}000$ steps. We refer to this configuration as the \\emph{evaluated model}.\n165:that is, $\\beta=0.2\\,(m_{256}-m_{16})+0.1\\,(m_{128}-m_{32})$; the middle width carries no weight, and the verdict depends only on how the two widest models compare with the two narrowest. Because every $m_d\\in[\\ln 250,\\ln 10{,}000]$, $|\\beta|\\le 0.3\\ln 40=1.107$, attained by a step profile; a delay falling log-linearly from the cap at $d=16$ to the floor at $d=256$ would give $0.922$. The interval has width $0.939$ and covers $42\\%$ of the attainable range $[-1.107,1.107]$. Its lower arm extends $0.565$ below the point estimate and its upper arm $0.374$ above.\n182:Slope of a log-linear cap-to-floor profile & $0.922$ \\\\\n327:Table~\\ref{tab:si-model} gives the width-dependent constants and parameter counts of the evaluated model. The parameter count follows from the layer shapes in the source: embeddings $(p+1)d$, positional embedding $3d$, attention projections $3d^2+d^2$ (no bias), MLP $4d^2+4d$ and $4d^2+d$, unembedding $pd$ (no bias), totalling $12d^2+(2p+9)d$. The initial-weight scale ($0.5$), the unembedding scale ($0.5$), the reference width ($128$), and both exponents ($0.5$) are constants in the source; the code comments state they were chosen without offline tuning.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f75a47ee-c82c-488f-8a2d-2df129d03c85","uuid":"3f91f4b6-1334-4f82-97cd-c717f4d9829f","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:55:55.434Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Agentic evidence audit feedback\n\n- generated_at: `2026-11-11T14:55:46.414137+00:00`\n- ok: `False`\n- verdict: `fail`\n- finding_count: `3`\n- audit_json_path: `work/results/t02/claude/20261111_085902/paper/paper_evidence_audit.json`\n- audit_markdown_path: `work/results/t02/claude/20261111_085902/paper/paper_evidence_audit.md`\n- raw_output_path: `work/results/t02/claude/20261111_085902/paper/paper_evidence_audit_raw.txt`\n\n## Summary\nNumbers, tables, figures and qualification facts match claims_grounding.json, paper_data.json and the evaluator sources; there are three narrow wording and sign inconsistencies, one of which makes a claim about when the decision rule was fixed that contradicts the Method section.\n\n## Findings\n- `rule-declared-in-advance-1` (Introduction (paragraph 2) vs Method (Test statistic and decision rule)): The Introduction says the protocol fixed the three-way rule in advance. The Method says the rule is the authors' own operationalization, that the evaluator emits only the interval and metrics, and that no claim is made about when the rule was fixed. The artifacts do not show the rule was declared before the evaluation.\n  - claim: The protocol declares in advance that the prediction is refuted if the interval lies at or above zero and inconclusive if it straddles zero.\n  - evidence: paper.tex Introduction vs Method 'the rule is our own operationalization ... we make no claim about when the rule was fixed'; claims_grounding.json claim[3] says the evaluator 'retains no verdict logic'; source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py computes only the slope and its interval and has no verdict logic; derived/resolution.json#verdict_from_interval_rule is produced by scripts/derive_resolution.py.\n  - requested_fix: Rewrite the Introduction sentence to say the authors apply a three-way rule to the evaluator's reported interval, and remove 'declares in advance' and any implication that the rule was pre-specified.\n- `loglinear-sign-1` (Results, Resolution of the design (Bounded dynamic range) and Table 'resolution'): The slope is defined as the slope of ln(delay) on log2(width), so a falling delay gives a negative slope of about -0.922. The text and table report the unsigned magnitude as a slope, which contradicts the paper's own sign convention.\n  - claim: a delay falling log-linearly from the cap at d=16 to the floor at d=256 would give 0.922; table row 'Slope of a log-linear cap-to-floor profile = 0.922'\n  - evidence: derived/resolution.json#loglinear_full_range_slope = 0.9222 (magnitude of ln(40)/4); scripts/derive_resolution.py line 36 comment 'slope of a profile falling log-linearly from the cap at 16 to the floor at 256'; paper.tex defines the verdict via the slope of ln(delay) vs log2(width) with negative meaning delay shrinks.\n  - requested_fix: Write the value as -0.922, or say 'magnitude 0.922' in both the text and the table row.\n- `constants-no-tuning-1` (Supplementary Information, Evaluated model: constants and parameter counts): The source comments say offline selection or execution was not possible only for the output-scaling, unembedding-init and hidden-exponent defaults. The comments for the initial-weight scale (0.5) and the reference width (128) say nothing about tuning. The paper attributes the 'without offline tuning' statement to the comments for all of these constants.\n  - claim: The initial-weight scale (0.5), the unembedding scale (0.5), the reference width (128), and both exponents (0.5) are constants in the source; the code comments state they were chosen without offline tuning.\n  - evidence: source_evidence/frozen_model/program.py: the comment on _INIT_SCALE says only 'Omnigrok-style smaller initial weight norm'; the comment on REF_WIDTH, OUT_EXP and UNEMBED_INIT says 'offline selection was not possible in this stage'; the comment on HIDDEN_EXP says 'a mild choice made without offline execution'. The development-plane variants V1-V3 were scored before V3 was selected as the evaluated model.\n  - requested_fix: Limit the 'without offline tuning' statement to the output-scaling, unembedding-init and hidden-exponent defaults as the comments state. For the initial-weight scale and reference width, say only that they are source constants, and qualify 'not tuned on it' in the Method accordingly.\n10 {\"claim\": \"Derived arithmetic from protocol constants (not from an evaluator metric): with delay floored at 250 and capped at 10,000 steps (ratio 40), OLS weights (-0.2,-0.1,0,0.1,0.2) over widths 16..256 give a maximum attainable |slope| of 0.3*ln(40)=1.107; a log-linear cap-to-floor profile gives 0.922.\", \"source_ids\": [], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_files\": [\"derived/resolution.json#max_abs_\n13 {\"claim\": \"Derived combinatorics from the four-seed design (not an evaluator metric): C(7,4)=35 distinct seed multisets; 4/4^4=1/64 of draws repeat one seed four times.\", \"source_ids\": [], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_files\": [\"derived/resolution.json#distinct_bootstrap_seed_multisets\", \"derived/resolution.json#bootstrap_draws_with_all_seeds_identical_prob\", \"source_evidence/workload/final_manif\n14 {\"claim\": \"Workload: modular addition modulo 97, 3,763 training and 5,646 held-out pairs, widths 16-256, 10,000 steps probed every 250, AdamW lr 1e-3, betas (0.9,0.98), weight decay 1.0, seeds 201-204; 2,000 bootstrap resamples and fit/generalization threshold 0.99 are read from the evaluator source.\", \"source_ids\": [], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_files\": [\"source_evidence/workload/final_manife\n15 {\"claim\": \"Parameter count 12d^2+(2p+9)d: 6,320 at d=16 to 838,400 at d=256 (derived arithmetic from the model source); multipliers (128/d)^0.5 = 2.83, 2.0, 1.41, 1.0, 0.71 (from source_evidence/frozen_model/program.py).\", \"source_ids\": [], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_files\": [\"source_evidence/frozen_model/program.py\", \"derived/resolution.json#params_by_width\", \"derived/resolution.json#hidden_a\n16 {\"claim\": \"Compute: 1,804 RPC calls (limit 2,200) over 22 episodes and 11,764 s elapsed in the final evaluation (source_evidence/final/final_manifest.json).\", \"source_ids\": [], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_files\": [\"source_evidence/final/final_manifest.json#compute_usage\", \"derived/resolution.json#compute\"]}\n33\n47:This paper reports a single, carefully scoped measurement of that kind. The setting is a one-layer transformer trained on modular addition modulo $p=97$ with a fixed training fraction, optimizer, and step limit, and the width $d$ is varied over $\\{16,32,64,128,256\\}$. The prediction under test is that the delay decreases with width, operationalized as the slope of $\\ln(\\text{delay})$ against $\\log_2 d$ being negative with a 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval entirely below zero. The protocol declares in advance that the prediction is \\emph{refuted} if the interval lies at or above zero and \\emph{inconclusive} if it straddles zero (Figure~\\ref{fig:overview}).\n103:The multiplier is $2.83, 2.0, 1.41, 1.0, 0.71$ at $d=16,\\dots,256$. The exponents ($0.5$) and the reference width ($128$) are fixed constants chosen before the final evaluation, not tuned on it. The training workload is dictated by the evaluator: AdamW with learning rate $10^{-3}$, $\\beta=(0.9,0.98)$, decoupled weight decay $1.0$, full-batch updates on all $3{,}763$ training pairs, and $10{,}000$ steps. We refer to this configuration as the \\emph{evaluated model}.\n165:that is, $\\beta=0.2\\,(m_{256}-m_{16})+0.1\\,(m_{128}-m_{32})$; the middle width carries no weight, and the verdict depends only on how the two widest models compare with the two narrowest. Because every $m_d\\in[\\ln 250,\\ln 10{,}000]$, $|\\beta|\\le 0.3\\ln 40=1.107$, attained by a step profile; a delay falling log-linearly from the cap at $d=16$ to the floor at $d=256$ would give $0.922$. The interval has width $0.939$ and covers $42\\%$ of the attainable range $[-1.107,1.107]$. Its lower arm extends $0.565$ below the point estimate and its upper arm $0.374$ above.\n182:Slope of a log-linear cap-to-floor profile & $0.922$ \\\\\n327:Table~\\ref{tab:si-model} gives the width-dependent constants and parameter counts of the evaluated model. The parameter count follows from the layer shapes in the source: embeddings $(p+1)d$, positional embedding $3d$, attention projections $3d^2+d^2$ (no bias), MLP $4d^2+4d$ and $4d^2+d$, unembedding $pd$ (no bias), totalling $12d^2+(2p+9)d$. The initial-weight scale ($0.5$), the unembedding scale ($0.5$), the reference width ($128$), and both exponents ($0.5$) are constants in the source; the code comments state they were chosen without offline tuning.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
