{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgwLNEo55F2hc5DcA98Fg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013dxskez86BrzcUuaXQbsoM","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'E'\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('paper_data.json'))\ngs=d['grounding_sources']\nprint(type(gs), len(gs))\nks=list(gs) if isinstance(gs,dict) else [g.get('id') for g in gs]\nprint([k for k in ks if 'final' in str(k) or 'workload' in str(k) or 'compute' in str(k) or 'manifest' in str(k)])\nc=json.load(open('claims_grounding.json'))['claims']\nfor i in (10,13,14,15,16): print(i,c[i])\nprint(c[0].keys())\nE\nsed -n 40,50p paper.tex; sed -n 100,104p paper.tex; sed -n 176,186p paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3898,"cache_read_input_tokens":47471,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3898},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f75a47ee-c82c-488f-8a2d-2df129d03c85","uuid":"88c30ca6-a1df-4e30-a8c5-3f31b0b248c4","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:55:58.181Z","request_id":"req_011CgwLNEH4UEt8fnV3zyuVq"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013dxskez86BrzcUuaXQbsoM","type":"tool_result","content":"<class 'dict'> 24\n['final.evidence.episodes', 'final.metric.censored_fraction', 'final.metric.combined_score', 'final.metric.control_max_val_acc', 'final.metric.grok_delay_log_slope', 'final.metric.grok_delay_slope_ci_high', 'final.metric.grok_delay_slope_ci_low', 'final.metric.missing_episodes', 'final.metric.reference_delay_log_slope', 'final.qualification.correct', 'final.qualification.outcome', 'final.qualification.status']\n10 {'claim': 'Derived arithmetic from protocol constants (not from an evaluator metric): with delay floored at 250 and capped at 10,000 steps (ratio 40), OLS weights (-0.2,-0.1,0,0.1,0.2) over widths 16..256 give a maximum attainable |slope| of 0.3*ln(40)=1.107; a log-linear cap-to-floor profile gives 0.922.', 'source_ids': [], 'citation_keys': [], 'evidence_files': ['derived/resolution.json#max_abs_slope_attainable', 'derived/resolution.json#loglinear_full_range_slope', 'source_evidence/workload/final_manifest.json#full', 'scripts/derive_resolution.py']}\n13 {'claim': 'Derived combinatorics from the four-seed design (not an evaluator metric): C(7,4)=35 distinct seed multisets; 4/4^4=1/64 of draws repeat one seed four times.', 'source_ids': [], 'citation_keys': [], 'evidence_files': ['derived/resolution.json#distinct_bootstrap_seed_multisets', 'derived/resolution.json#bootstrap_draws_with_all_seeds_identical_prob', 'source_evidence/workload/final_manifest.json#seeds']}\n14 {'claim': 'Workload: modular addition modulo 97, 3,763 training and 5,646 held-out pairs, widths 16-256, 10,000 steps probed every 250, AdamW lr 1e-3, betas (0.9,0.98), weight decay 1.0, seeds 201-204; 2,000 bootstrap resamples and fit/generalization threshold 0.99 are read from the evaluator source.', 'source_ids': [], 'citation_keys': [], 'evidence_files': ['source_evidence/workload/final_manifest.json', 'source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py', 'derived/resolution.json#n_train_pairs']}\n15 {'claim': 'Parameter count 12d^2+(2p+9)d: 6,320 at d=16 to 838,400 at d=256 (derived arithmetic from the model source); multipliers (128/d)^0.5 = 2.83, 2.0, 1.41, 1.0, 0.71 (from source_evidence/frozen_model/program.py).', 'source_ids': [], 'citation_keys': [], 'evidence_files': ['source_evidence/frozen_model/program.py', 'derived/resolution.json#params_by_width', 'derived/resolution.json#hidden_and_output_multipliers']}\n16 {'claim': 'Compute: 1,804 RPC calls (limit 2,200) over 22 episodes and 11,764 s elapsed in the final evaluation (source_evidence/final/final_manifest.json).', 'source_ids': [], 'citation_keys': [], 'evidence_files': ['source_evidence/final/final_manifest.json#compute_usage', 'derived/resolution.json#compute']}\ndict_keys(['claim', 'source_ids', 'citation_keys'])\nThe evaluation itself was valid, so the result is a valid but inconclusive measurement rather than a failed run; the interval comes from only four seed clusters and its coverage is not guaranteed. We state what the result cannot say and which measurements would resolve it.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\nIn small algorithmic tasks such as modular addition, networks often fit the training split almost immediately and generalize to held-out pairs only after much longer training \\citep{Power2022GrokkingGB}. Several accounts of this delay have been proposed: a mechanistic account in which a generalizing circuit forms gradually under weight decay \\citep{Nanda2023ProgressMF}, a loss-landscape and weight-norm account \\citep{Liu2022OmnigrokGB}, a circuit-efficiency account \\citep{Varma2023ExplainingGT}, and a lazy-to-rich account of training dynamics \\citep{Kumar2023GrokkingAT}. How the delay depends on \\emph{model size} is a more practical question: it constrains these accounts and informs which model sizes are worth using in experiments on the phenomenon. A capacity-based reading predicts that wider models memorize sooner, and, if generalization time is less sensitive to width, that the delay changes systematically with width.\n\nThis paper reports a single, carefully scoped measurement of that kind. The setting is a one-layer transformer trained on modular addition modulo $p=97$ with a fixed training fraction, optimizer, and step limit, and the width $d$ is varied over $\\{16,32,64,128,256\\}$. The prediction under test is that the delay decreases with width, operationalized as the slope of $\\ln(\\text{delay})$ against $\\log_2 d$ being negative with a 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval entirely below zero. The protocol declares in advance that the prediction is \\emph{refuted} if the interval lies at or above zero and \\emph{inconclusive} if it straddles zero (Figure~\\ref{fig:overview}).\n\nThe outcome is inconclusive. The sealed evaluation, run on four seeds at each of five widths, gives a slope of $-0.020$ with 95\\% interval $[-0.585, 0.354]$. The evaluation was valid in the sense that it completed and passed the evaluator's validity checks (the evaluator labels it \\texttt{qualified}); this status concerns the integrity of the measurement and does not mean that the width prediction was supported. Because a flat answer is easy to misread, much of this paper is about \\emph{why} the measurement is inconclusive and how large an effect it could have resolved.\n\n\\item \\emph{Width-aware output scaling.} The unembedding weights are additionally halved at initialization, and the logits are multiplied by $m_d=(128/d)^{1/2}$.\n\\item \\emph{Hidden-layer reparametrization.} Each hidden matrix is written $W=m_d V$ with the same multiplier $m_d$, and $V$ is initialized as $W_{\\mathrm{init}}/m_d$ so that the function at initialization is unchanged. The optimizer updates $V$, and decoupled weight decay acts on $V$. The code comments state the intent: under Adam-type updates $V$ moves at about the learning rate per step, so $W$ moves at about $m_d$ times the learning rate, which is meant to compensate for width in the effective step size.\n\\end{enumerate}\nThe multiplier is $2.83, 2.0, 1.41, 1.0, 0.71$ at $d=16,\\dots,256$. The exponents ($0.5$) and the reference width ($128$) are fixed constants chosen before the final evaluation, not tuned on it. The training workload is dictated by the evaluator: AdamW with learning rate $10^{-3}$, $\\beta=(0.9,0.98)$, decoupled weight decay $1.0$, full-batch updates on all $3{,}763$ training pairs, and $10{,}000$ steps. We refer to this configuration as the \\emph{evaluated model}.\n\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\nQuantity & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nDelay floor / cap (steps) & $250$ / $10{,}000$ \\\\\nLargest attainable $|\\text{slope}|$ (step profile) & $1.107$ \\\\\nSlope of a log-linear cap-to-floor profile & $0.922$ \\\\\nInterval width & $0.939$ \\\\\nInterval width as fraction of $[-1.107, 1.107]$ & $0.42$ \\\\\nInterval arms below / above the point estimate & $0.565$ / $0.374$ \\\\\nPer-doubling delay factor: point [interval] & $0.980$ [$0.557$, $1.425$] \\\\","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f75a47ee-c82c-488f-8a2d-2df129d03c85","uuid":"29cb4681-e3ec-43e2-9cdf-f0f357e25304","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:55:58.308Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"<class 'dict'> 24\n['final.evidence.episodes', 'final.metric.censored_fraction', 'final.metric.combined_score', 'final.metric.control_max_val_acc', 'final.metric.grok_delay_log_slope', 'final.metric.grok_delay_slope_ci_high', 'final.metric.grok_delay_slope_ci_low', 'final.metric.missing_episodes', 'final.metric.reference_delay_log_slope', 'final.qualification.correct', 'final.qualification.outcome', 'final.qualification.status']\n10 {'claim': 'Derived arithmetic from protocol constants (not from an evaluator metric): with delay floored at 250 and capped at 10,000 steps (ratio 40), OLS weights (-0.2,-0.1,0,0.1,0.2) over widths 16..256 give a maximum attainable |slope| of 0.3*ln(40)=1.107; a log-linear cap-to-floo